<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Nothing in this project has ever declared entitlements at all - there was no
	     .entitlements file, and CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS wasn't set in project.yml. The
	     app's own JIT pre-flight check (ios_jit_is_permitted() in CemuBridge.mm) has
	     been failing consistently on device: "mmap(MAP_JIT, executable at map time)
	     refused (errno 22 - Invalid argument), even though CS_DEBUGGED is set." Being
	     debugged (CS_DEBUGGED, which SideStore/AltStore-style sideloading sets via a
	     momentary debugger attach at launch) is necessary but may not be sufficient on
	     its own - a JIT-capable process is also expected to declare intent via these
	     entitlements, which the kernel's mmap(MAP_JIT) enforcement can check in
	     addition to the debugged flag. Adding them is the standard, correct thing a
	     JIT-using iOS app should do either way.

	     Said plainly: this is a real gap worth closing, not a confirmed fix. Whether it
	     actually unlocks MAP_JIT depends on how the specific sideloading tool signs and
	     re-signs the app afterward (SideStore/AltStore/LiveContainer each handle
	     entitlement injection differently, and Apple restricts CS_ALLOW_JIT-adjacent
	     entitlements even for signed apps outside specific approved cases) - none of
	     which this repo controls. If this doesn't change anything on device, the
	     existing pre-flight check keeps doing its job: falling back to the interpreter
	     safely rather than handing the recompiler a mapping already known to crash. -->
	<key>get-task-allow</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection</key>
	<true/>
	<key>dynamic-codesigning</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Raises the memory iOS lets MuffinEMU use before terminating it, the same request
	     MeloCafe's own app makes. It matters most on 6 GB devices like the A12Z iPad Pro,
	     where the default limit sits close to what a retail title needs. Honoured when the
	     signature carries it (the ad-hoc signed IPA always does). -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- NOT requesting com.apple.developer.kernel.extended-virtual-addressing, and this
	     is a deliberate absence rather than an oversight.

	     It was added in v4.5 to help the recompiler reserve its arena in one piece, and
	     it broke JIT entirely: StikDebug stopped listing MuffinEMU at all. It is a
	     RESTRICTED entitlement - it only takes effect through a provisioning profile that
	     grants it - and when a re-signer meets an entitlement its profile does not
	     authorise, the entitlements get dropped. Including get-task-allow, which is the
	     one every JIT enabler looks for. An app that asks for too much is indistinguishable
	     from an app that asked for nothing.

	     It bought nothing either: with it present the arena still only reached ~1 GB, so
	     the larger reservations were failing regardless. Trading the recompiler for a
	     reservation that never succeeded is a straight loss. The arena ladder handles a
	     failed reservation properly now, which is the part that actually mattered. -->
</dict>
</plist>
